  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "$9{m}^{4}+4{n}^{10}=$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the given expression is a sum of squares, since\n\n$$\n9{m}^{4}+4{n}^{10}={(3{m}^{2})}^{2}+{(2{n}^{5})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo factor a sum of squares, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{m}^{4}+4{n}^{10} & =9{m}^{4}-(-4{n}^{10}) \\\\  & =9{m}^{4}-(4{\\text{i}}^{2}{n}^{10}) \\\\  & ={(3{m}^{2})}^{2}-{(2\\text{i}{n}^{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =(3{m}^{2}+2\\text{i}{n}^{5})(3{m}^{2}-2\\text{i}{n}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
